## Q2: And what about equal and eqls

## Answer 2:

In Ruby, the two methods you are referring to are equal? and eql? (note the exact spelling, as there is no eqls method in Ruby). [1] 
The primary difference is that equal? checks for object identity (if two variables point to the exact same spot in memory), while eql? checks for strict type and value equality (often used for hash keys). [2, 3, 4, 5, 6] 
------------------------------
## Comparison of All Four Ruby Equality Methods

| Method | Focus | Strictness | Common Use Case |
|---|---|---|---|
| == | Value | Loose numeric types | Everyday conditional logic |
| === | Belonging | Case / Set membership | case/when statements |
| eql? | Value + Type | Strict data types | Hash key generation [1] |
| equal? | Identity | Ultimate strictness | Memory address checks |

------------------------------
## How equal? Works (Identity Equality)
The equal? method determines if two objects are the exact same entity in your system's memory. It compares the internal object_id of the items. Even if two items look identical and have the same data, equal? will return false if they live in different memory slots. [7, 8, 9] 

str1 = "ruby"
str2 = "ruby"

str1 == str2       # => true  (They have the same text)
str1.equal?(str2)  # => false (They are two separate objects in memory)

str3 = str1
str1.equal?(str3)  # => true  (They point to the exact same memory address)

Note: You should never override the equal? method in your custom classes.
------------------------------
## How eql? Works (Strict Type Equality)
The eql? method checks if two objects have the same value and the exact same data type. Unlike ==, it does not allow implicit matching across different numeric types (like matching an Integer to a Float). [10, 11, 12, 13] 

5 == 5.0     # => true  (Value equality ignores the type difference)5.eql?(5.0)  # => false (Integer vs Float: Different data types) [1]

## Why eql? Matters: Hash Keys
Ruby uses eql? behind the scenes to determine how keys are stored and retrieved in a Hash. If two keys do not return true for eql?, they are treated as completely different keys, even if == says they are equal.

weights = {}
weights[5] = "Five"
weights[5.0] = "Five Point Zero"
# Because 5.eql?(5.0) is false, Ruby creates two separate keys:# weights => {5=>"Five", 5.0=>"Five Point Zero"}

